/**
\page mp3x_internals mp3x frame analyzer - internals

The GTK4 frame analyzer has four source files:

- mp3x_core.c: the analysis engine. It does not use GTK.
- mp3x_plot.c: the drawing code, with Cairo.
- mp3x_session.c: opening and closing one input file.
- mp3x_ui.c: the application, the window and the menus.

The mp3x(1) manual page describes what the user sees: the graphs, the display
controls and the keys. This page describes the code.

\section mp3x_internals_lifetime Architecture and lifetime

\c Mp3xDriver holds the state of the process and of the user interface. It
uses the application and the window, but does not own them. It owns the
current session, the menu model, the CSS provider, the view settings, the
slots that identify pending dialog requests, the session generation counter,
and the exit status flag for analysis failures. Once this flag is set, it stays
set.

\c Mp3xSession is one open input file. It owns its \c lame_t, the path, the
display name, and the playback state. To close a session, the code first removes
its idle source, then shuts down the decoder of the engine, calls
\c close_infile(), closes the LAME instance and frees the strings. Before it
opens another file, the code restores the frontend globals as they were at
startup. So options from the command line do not apply to a file that the user
opens later in the GUI.

Each file dialog request owns a strong reference to the driver, a dialog, a
cancellable, and one matching \c g_application_hold(). The code that completes
the request releases all four. Each idle source owns a strong reference to the
driver, the session pointer and the session generation from the time it was
created. Its destroy notify frees them.

\note An async callback must match <b>both</b> the state epoch of the driver and
the session generation before it changes the session state. This is why a
dialog can complete safely after the file it was opened for is closed.

The GUI checks a selected \c GFile before it closes the current session. So if
the selected file cannot be used, the user keeps the file that is already open.
A successful \c Mp3xPrevalidateResult owns its three strings until they are
cleared or taken over by the new session.

\section mp3x_internals_transport Playback and end of input

The end of the input and the end of the display are different states. If the
code mixes them up, the last frames of a file are lost.

For PCM input, the first mp3x_core_step() that returns zero has already done
four things: it shifted the ring, called \c lame_encode_flush() once, passed
all of its output to HIP, and decoded at most one queued frame. The session then
calls mp3x_core_drain_step() exactly <tt>READ_AHEAD - 1</tt> times. The first
of these calls collect the HIP output that the flush left in the queue. When
HIP needs more data, the calls shift in invalid placeholder frames. Drain steps
do not read input, encode, or update the statistics.

The display is complete only when the last real frame reaches the display
position. So Play, repeated Step, and Last/Advance all end on the same last
frame, with the matching decoded frame.

\c frames_done counts only real analyzed frames. Every plotting slot of a real
frame stores <tt>totbits == sum(mainbits[gr][ch])</tt>. If the engine returns a
negative result, the session stops and the process exit status becomes 1. A
later successful open does not reset it.

\section mp3x_internals_cli Command line

mp3x parses the command line before it creates the \c GtkApplication. If the
parser shows information (help, version, license, usage, long help or the genre
list), mp3x prints it once and exits with 0, without a window. If parsing or
opening fails, mp3x exits with a non-zero status. A session opened from the
command line is installed when GTK activates. A file opened later in the GUI
uses clean default settings. It does not use the options from the command
line again.

\section mp3x_internals_plot Plotting and export

The titles on the screen use a Pango layout created from the drawing widget. So
they use the font map, font, text direction, DPI and text scaling of the
desktop. PNG files use a fixed PangoCairo \c "Sans 11" layout instead, so a
saved image looks the same on every desktop. Each graph leaves out the measured
height of the title when it maps its data. The drawing areas have accessible
labels.

Every PNG writer checks the image surface, the Cairo context and the result of
\c cairo_surface_write_to_png(). Save and Export show the returned error. They
do not report success after a failure.

\section mp3x_internals_hooks Test-only instrumentation

Normal builds do not read any of the environment variables below. The test hooks
are compiled only when \c MP3X_ENABLE_TEST_HOOKS is defined:

\code
./configure --enable-mp3x CPPFLAGS=-DMP3X_ENABLE_TEST_HOOKS
make
\endcode

\warning On purpose, there is no configure option for this. The define is the
only way to enable the hooks, and it is meant to be inconvenient. The
regression checks below include a check that a normal binary contains none of
the \c MP3X_ strings.

Only a build with this define reads:

- \c MP3X_START_PAUSED: analyze one step, then wait.
- \c MP3X_STEP_TO_END: use repeated Step while paused, until the end.
- \c MP3X_ADVANCE_TO_END: use Last/Advance to go to the end.
- \c MP3X_QUIT_WHEN_DONE: quit when the display is complete, or after an
  injected failure.
- \c MP3X_FAIL_AFTER=N: inject an analysis failure after N real frames.
- \c MP3X_STATE_REPORT=path: write a report with the last frame, the drain
  state, the \c totbits check, the offset of the matching decoded frame, and the
  peak of the decoded PCM.
- \c MP3X_SHOT, \c MP3X_SHOT_RESYNTH, \c MP3X_SHOT_MDCT, \c MP3X_SHOT_MDCT1,
  \c MP3X_SHOT_PSY, \c MP3X_SHOT_PSY1, \c MP3X_SHOT_SFB, \c MP3X_SHOT_SFB1,
  \c MP3X_SHOT_COMPOSITE: save all nine plots of the last frame.
- \c MP3X_CHANNEL, \c MP3X_MS, \c MP3X_DIFF, \c MP3X_SOURCE, \c MP3X_NOSFBLINES,
  \c MP3X_KBFLAG: set the display options.

\section mp3x_internals_regression Suggested regression checks

-# Build a normal and an instrumented binary. The changed files build without
   warnings.
-# Run every informational command-line option and one invalid option, each
   with a time limit.
-# Use WAV, AIFF and MP3 inputs, both shorter and longer than \c READ_AHEAD.
-# Check that Play, repeated Step, and Last/Advance end on the same last frame.
-# Check every reported \c totbits against the four \c mainbits fields. Every
   last displayed frame must have a matching decoded frame. For a silent input,
   a decoded PCM peak of zero is correct.
-# Select an empty file, a directory, a missing file, an unreadable file and a
   file that is not a regular file.
-# Repeat open, recent, close, cancel and save many times under the leak checker
   of the platform.
-# Use all nine PNG save options, also with a destination that cannot be written.
-# Use the channel, source, difference, SFB, subblock and spectrum controls, the
   statistics, the playback keys, full screen, and replacing the open file.
-# Check that a normal binary contains none of the \c MP3X_ hook strings.

*/
